**Coding**

All three snapshots are one record group on `HB2756 (2026)`:

- **Bill overview** — `own-act` / `record` / `sponsor`, `actor_quote` "Sponsors: Griffin (Prime)".
- **Chapter 111 text** — carries the `provision_quote` (the cost-coverage characteristics the commission *may* establish, with schedules protecting non-EHLF customers from subsidizing).
- **House final reading** — `record` / `vote`, `actor_quote` "GRIFFIN Y", `tally_quote` "Passed 39-16-5-0-0" (16 of 60 No, so not near-unanimous).

**Chair: BLANK `compound-partial`.** The act requires semiannual interconnection reports and cost-of-service studies and authorizes the corporation commission to make extra-high-load-factor customers cover their own infrastructure costs. That evidences only the transparency clause of rung 4 — nothing in the act streamlines permitting — and the permissive commission authority falls short of rung 2's bar on passing *any* data center infrastructure costs to residential customers. Per V4.1 I read the operative sections, not the Sec. 6 legislative findings, which state the anti-cost-shift purpose more strongly than the enacted text does.

`needs_source` lists Griffin's own words tying HB2756 to data centers and permitting, plus any siting/permitting instrument she acted on. No candidate quotes: none of the three sources contains her words.
